//! Sparse matrices on core's `Array`: compressed rows (`Csr`), compressed
//! columns (`Csc`) and triplets (`Coo`), their products and conversions,
//! Krylov solvers (`cg`, `gmres`, `bicgstab`) with preconditioners
//! (`Jacobi`, `ilu0`, `ic0`) that also take matrix-free operators, and
//! direct factorizations (`cholesky`, `lu`) after a fill-reducing order.
//! Values are f64. Bad input throws a `SparseError` that says what to
//! call instead; nothing panics except the operators, which cannot
//! throw and panic on a shape mismatch as core's do.
//!
//! Pure Nabi, so every target, wasm included, gets the same results.
//!
//! ## Tutorial
//!
//! A matrix with a million rows does not fit in memory as a dense array,
//! but most such matrices are almost all zeros: each unknown couples to a
//! few neighbours. `sparse` stores only the nonzeros. It works on core's
//! `Vector<f64>` ([Arrays and
//! numerics](https://docs.nabi-lang.org/latest/learn/21-arrays-and-numerics.html)),
//! like [`linalg`](https://packages.nabi-lang.org/docs/linalg/latest),
//! which handles dense matrices and whose tutorial covers solving, least
//! squares and eigenvalues.
//!
//! ### Sparse matrices
//!
//! Build a matrix from (row, column, value) triplets with `Coo`, then
//! convert it to `Csr` for arithmetic and solving.
//!
//! ```nabi
//! let c = sparse::Coo::new([4, 4]);
//! for i in 0..4 {
//!     try c.push(i, i, 4.0);
//!     if i + 1 < 4 {
//!         try c.push(i, i + 1, -1.0);
//!         try c.push(i + 1, i, -1.0);
//!     }
//! }
//! let a = c.to_csr();
//! println(f"{a.nnz()} nonzeros in a {a.shape():?} matrix");
//! println(try a.matvec([1, 1, 1, 1]));
//! # assert(a.nnz() == 10);
//! # assert((try a.matvec([1, 1, 1, 1])).to_list() == [3.0, 2.0, 2.0, 3.0]);
//! ```
//!
//! ```text
//! 10 nonzeros in a [4, 4] matrix
//! [3.0, 2.0, 2.0, 3.0]
//! ```
//!
//! ### Solve a large sparse system
//!
//! The heat equation on a square plate, discretized on a 100 x 100 grid,
//! gives 10 000 unknowns and a matrix with five nonzeros per row.
//! `sparse::diags` builds the one-dimensional difference matrix from its
//! diagonals, and `kron` combines two of them into the two-dimensional one.
//! `sparse::cg` solves it iteratively with conjugate gradients; `ic0` (an
//! incomplete Cholesky factorization) cuts the number of iterations.
//!
//! ```nabi
//! use core::array::Vector;
//!
//! let n = 100;
//! let t = try sparse::diags([[2.0], [-1.0], [-1.0]], [0, 1, -1], [n, n]);
//! let i = sparse::eye(n);
//! let a = sparse::kron(i, t) + sparse::kron(t, i);
//! let b = Vector::<f64>::ones([n * n]);
//!
//! let plain = try sparse::cg(a, b);
//! let pre = try sparse::cg(a, b, pre: try sparse::ic0(a));
//! println(f"{a.nnz()} nonzeros; cg {plain.iterations} iterations, with ic0 {pre.iterations}");
//!
//! let x = try (try sparse::cholesky(a)).solve(b);
//! println(f"direct and iterative agree: {linalg::norm(x .- pre.x) / linalg::norm(x) < 1e-6}");
//! println(f"hottest point {x.max() ?? 0.0:.2}");
//! # assert(a.nnz() == 49600 && plain.iterations == 187 && pre.iterations == 79);
//! # assert(linalg::norm(x .- pre.x) / linalg::norm(x) < 1e-6);
//! # assert(((x.max() ?? 0.0) - 751.34).abs() < 0.005);
//! ```
//!
//! ```text
//! 49600 nonzeros; cg 187 iterations, with ic0 79
//! direct and iterative agree: true
//! hottest point 751.34
//! ```
//!
//! The iterative solvers take a tolerance (`rtol:`), an iteration limit and
//! any `Operator`, including a function that applies the matrix without
//! storing it (`sparse::operator`). `sparse::cholesky` and `sparse::lu` are
//! direct solvers that reorder the matrix to keep the factors sparse.
//!
//! ### What next
//!
//! - [`linalg`](https://packages.nabi-lang.org/docs/linalg/latest): dense
//!   matrices, factorizations, least squares, eigenvalues and the SVD.
//! - [`stats`](https://packages.nabi-lang.org/docs/stats/latest):
//!   distributions, tests and regression.
//! - The rest of this package: GMRES, BiCGSTAB and preconditioners.
//!

